  "kind": "excel-differs-from-math",
  "title": "LOG(number, base) always divides RN53(RN64(LN(number) / LN(base))), even for base 10",
  "statement": "In Excel 16.0 builds 20131 and 20430, omitting base (LOG(number)) routes to LOG10 and returns exact integers on powers of 10 (LOG(1000) = 3, LOG(0.1) = -1), whereas passing an explicit base (including base = 10 or 2) evaluates the staged x87 quotient RN53(RN64(LN(number) / LN(base))). Consequently, LOG(1000, 10) returns 2.9999999999999996 (0x4007ffffffffffff) and LOG(0.1, 10) returns -0.9999999999999998 (0xbfeffffffffffffe). LOG(1, base < 1) publishes +0, and base = 1 returns #DIV/0!. All 25,233 full-bank LOG rows match.",
  "status": "measured",
  "basis": "Measured in CalcFunc-Evidence runs/w111-log-omitted-base-20260929/answers-log.json.gz (Excel 16.0 build 20430) and matched on 25,233/25,233 full-bank LOG rows.",
